      "text": "1. **Define the Variables:**\n   Let \\( n \\) be a two-digit integer represented as \\( 10a + b \\), where \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are its digits and \\( a \\neq 0 \\).\n\n2. **Switch the Digits:**\n   The reversed integer of \\( n \\) is \\( 10b + a \\).\n\n3. **Formulate the Equation:**\n   According to the problem, switching the digits of \\( n \\) results in an integer that, when 3 is added to it, equals \\( 2n \\). Therefore, we can write the equation:\n   \\[\n   10b + a + 3 = 2(10a + b)\n   \\]\n\n4. **Simplify the Equation:**\n   Expand and simplify the right-hand side:\n   \\[\n   10b + a + 3 = 20a + 2b\n   \\]\n\n5. **Rearrange to Isolate Variables:**\n   Move all terms involving \\( b \\) and \\( a \\) to one side of the equation:\n   \\[\n   10b - 2b + a - 20a = -3\n   \\]\n   Simplify:\n   \\[\n   8b - 19a = -3\n   \\]\n\n6. **Solve for Integer Solutions:**\n   We need to find integer values for \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) that satisfy this equation. Rearrange to solve for \\( b \\):\n   \\[\n   8b = 19a - 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b = \\frac{19a - 3}{8}\n   \\]\n   For \\( b \\) to be an integer, \\( 19a - 3 \\) must be divisible by 8. We check values of \\( a \\) starting from the smallest non-zero digit (1):\n\n   - If \\( a = 1 \\):\n     \\[\n     19(1) - 3 = 16 \\quad \\text{(divisible by 8)}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     b = \\frac{16}{8} = 2\n     \\]\n     So, \\( n = 10a + b = 10(1) + 2 = 12 \\).\n\n7. **Verify the Solution:**\n   Switch the digits of 12 to get 21. Adding 3 results in \\( 21 + 3 = 24 \\), which is indeed \\( 2 \\times 12 = 24 \\).\n\nTherefore, the smallest two-digit integer \\( n \\) that satisfies the condition is \\(\\boxed{12}\\).",
